  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nNow - Sunday Night...\n\nDry NW flow aloft combined with high pressure at the surface will \ncontinue to keep things dry through the end of the weekend. The \nbiggest story will be the cold overnight lows as good radiational \ncooling allows for what should be the coldest night of the season so \nfar. 07/13z NBM probabilities for temps 32 or below has some fairly \nwidespread values over 70% meaning that a fairly widespread freeze \nseems likely. Ongoing Freeze Warning for tonight looks good for most \nof the area. Some of the typically milder urban areas may not quite \nget there, but even then, frost looks like a good bet. Will also \nlikely have some river valley fog once again, and with temperatures \nlikely below freezing in spots folks will want to pay attention for \na few slick spots if driving through dense fog where temps are below \nfreezing.\n\nTemperatures do begin to recover a bit for Sunday with highs back \nnear normal around 60 degrees. Sunday night will be chilly once \nagain, but perhaps a bit milder than tonight. Most lows should be in \nthe low and mid 30s, and may end up needing one more Frost Advisory \nhere which will likely close out the Frost/Freeze headlines for the \nseason. Collaboration on that will have to be done Monday with UK Ag \nand surrounding offices.\n\nMonday - Wednesday...\n\nDry NW flow aloft will hold over the area for the early to middle \npart of the week. This will allow for continued dry conditions and \nmoderating temperatures. There will be a weak shortwave moving out \nof the Red River Valley into the low/mid Mississippi River Valley \nlater Tuesday night into Wednesday, however this lead features looks \nto have no notable impact on our sensible weather, at least \ninitially. After highs in the low and mid 60s for Monday, Tuesday \nbrings temps in the mid 60s to near 70. Similar temps are expected \nfor Wednesday, but will back off a degree or two given increased sky \ncover ahead of the next system. Overall, these temperatures are \nconsidered a bit above normal values around 60 for this time of \nyear. Morning lows will be in the 30s to low 40s, and will likely \nsee some patches of frost, especially in the eastern cooler valleys.\n\nWednesday Night - Saturday...\n\nAn interesting evolution of the eastern CONUS upper pattern takes \nshape as we move into the later half of the work week and first part \nof the weekend. We'll see some likely phasing between the polar and \nsubtropical jets as shortwave energy ejects across the Plains and \ntaps into the sub-tropical wind energy and moisture. This will \nresult in a developing strong system over our area, with a mature \nstorm then progressing up the east Coast into New England late week. \nThe exact timing and placement of this phase near our area will have \nan impact on how much precipitation we see later Wednesday night \nthrough Friday. Whether there will be enough lingering low level \nmoisture far enough west over our area to support snow chances on \nFriday remains to be seen, but did notice a couple of rogue ensemble \nmembers from the 07/00z suite do show some light snowfall during \nthat late week stretch. The more likely scenario is that the phase \nhappens late enough that the system is too far east with bulk of \nthe moisture, and that cold air doesn't quite catch up in time to \nallow for snow. We'll monitor trends through the week.\n\nLook for a notable cool down the 2nd half of the week into the \nweekend. Highs on Friday are only expected to be in the upper 40s to \naround 50, with low and mid 50s expected for Thursday and Saturday. \nLater Friday evening into Friday night would be the best chance for \na few festive flakes assuming there is enough low level moisture \nstill lingering to work with.",
